
Post-Retirement Speculation / Post-Commit Speculation : Is the operative term here




 pre-commit  : before instructions have retired from ROB
 post-commit : after instructions have retired from ROB
 
 You can fake SC with RMO pre-commit fairly easily. That is, retire the load/stores speculatively and let ROB retire in-order. If you can hide load/store stalls with this
 there is no issue. This will require usage of pre-commit store buffers. 


 The question is, if you cannot hide the latency with this technique then what do you do:

 The "invise-fence" paper argues that here you can do one of 2 things:
   - Post-commit (post-retirement) store buffers : put the stores in a separate store buffer and let them retire in-order. If intermediate coherence stalls from other
    cores show up, then you know you messed up and therefore have to rollback. Maintain rollback data.

   - Bulk Commit : WIP


 -- Keep in mind, when you detect ordering violations, you have the opportunity to push back on the Violators instead of rolling back

